.TH DUMP 1 "October 4, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dump \- read the bytes at a virtual address, resolved through their section
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dump
.I hex-vaddr
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RB [ \-\-len= n ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reports the bytes stored at a virtual address, together with the section that maps
it and the file offset the bytes begin at.
.PP
.B a2o
answers which byte of the file an address lands at and stops there. The next
question is always what is stored in it, and the answer is not a plain
.BR dd (1)
window. An address resolves to a
.I section
, and the dump starts at the address and stops at that section's own end. Reading
past the boundary would silently blend two sections \- 
.B __cstring
into
.B __const
, or the tail of
.B __text
into whatever the linker packed after it \- and a reader comparing those bytes
against a hex editor's window would be looking at two different things without
being told.
.SH THREE ANSWERS
An address can reach a byte, be mapped with no byte, or be in no slice at all.
All three are answers, and the exit status follows the taxonomy every tool here
uses.
.B zerofill
(addresses in
.BR __bss ,
.B __noptrbss
or
.BR __PAGEZERO ,
and the tail of a segment past its
.BR filesize )
means the address is mapped in memory and no byte of it exists in the file.
.B mapped
false means no slice maps the address at all. Both exit 1, because the tool
answered the question and the answer was no.
.PP
.B __bss
is the case that makes this a separate tool rather than an option on
.BR a2o :
converting such an address to an offset must refuse, and printing the two refused
cases as one would send a patch script to
.B __TEXT
for a byte that lives in zeroed memory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-len= n
How many bytes to read, at most. Default 64, and capped at 1048576. This is a
request, not an override: the dump also stops at the section's end and at the
slice's own extent, because on a universal binary the bytes after a slice belong
to the next architecture. The
.B truncated
field says when the request was not met in full.
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Read one architecture by name:
.BR x86_64 ,
.BR arm64 ,
.BR arm64e ,
.BR arm64_32 ,
.BR ppc ,
.BR ppc64 ,
.BR arm
or
.BR i386 .
A universal binary maps
.B __TEXT
at 0x100000000 in
.I every
slice, so without this flag the first slice is read.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read. May also be given as the second positional argument, because
the first is unambiguously an address.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send all prose to standard error.
Each row carries its address as a
.B "0x..."
string: a 64\-bit virtual address does not survive a JSON number.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given.
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_EXPLORER_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Bytes were read.
.TP
.B 1
The address was mapped with no byte, or in no slice. A negative result, not a
failure.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including an address that is not hexadecimal with an
.B 0x
prefix or a
.B \-\-len
that is not a positive whole number.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read or parsed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
dump 0x100001000 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go
dump \-\-len=32 0x100085c30 /usr/local/go/bin/go
dump \-\-json 0x100001000 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go | jq '.data.lines[0].hex'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR a2o (1),
.BR o2a (1),
.BR findliteral (1),
.BR mapliteral (1),
.BR describe (1),
.BR xxd (1)
